              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Washington State Department of Social Services, the case concerns whether a state representative payee\u2019s use of Social Security benefits to pay a beneficiary\u2019s current maintenance violates the anti\u2011alienation provision of 42\u202fU.S.C.\u202f\u00a7\u202f407(a)."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "State",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "f00dbdec4b133a652495a9bec0ea67ee1c61f236b1e5bd61246dce141c972b3b",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-f00dbdec4b13-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 37,
              "start": 37
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to State, the Department of Social and Health Services argues that the anti\u2011alienation provision does not apply because the department is acting as a properly supervised representative payee."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Overview",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
  },
  "party_positions": [
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Washington State Department of Social Services",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "f00dbdec4b133a652495a9bec0ea67ee1c61f236b1e5bd61246dce141c972b3b",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-f00dbdec4b13-0000-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 5,
                "start": 5
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Washington State Department of Social Services, the department is duly recognized as a representative payee subject to supervision, required to submit constant and annual reports, and is audited, acting in accordance with 405(j)."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "United States",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "f00dbdec4b133a652495a9bec0ea67ee1c61f236b1e5bd61246dce141c972b3b",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-f00dbdec4b13-0000-0002"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 17,
                "start": 17
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to United States, the United States (amicus) states that the use of Social Security benefits to pay for current maintenance needs by a beneficiary or a parent as representative payee is permitted under the Social Security Act."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "State",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "f00dbdec4b133a652495a9bec0ea67ee1c61f236b1e5bd61246dce141c972b3b",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-f00dbdec4b13-0002-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 37,
                "start": 37
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "The State argues that \u00a7\u202f407(a) does not apply to it because it is acting properly as a representative payee."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "State of Washington",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "f00dbdec4b133a652495a9bec0ea67ee1c61f236b1e5bd61246dce141c972b3b",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-f00dbdec4b13-0003-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 53,
                "start": 53
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to State of Washington, the State of Washington pays thousands of dollars for children with psychiatric needs, providing significant amounts of money rather than the minimum."
        }
